    <item>
      <title>Learning Japanese Part I</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/72d4e91/</link>
      <pubDate>Sun, 26 Jul 2026 00:00:01 -0400</pubDate>
      
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/72d4e91/</guid>
      <description>&lt;aside class=&#34;yh-pullquote&#34; style=&#34;--yh-pullquote-w: 50%;&#34;&gt;
  &lt;div class=&#34;yh-pullquote-text&#34;&gt;
    The goal of mathematics is harmony in truth, and the goal of art is harmony in beauty; emotion is at work in both.
  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class=&#34;yh-pullquote-attrib&#34;&gt;— Kiyoshi Oka&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I found an excellent Japanese Grammar text by Tae Kim, so I decided to write down my notes while studying it. I am a near complete beginner in Japanese, but I know some basics such as the hiraganas and katanagas. I am also a native mandarin speaker, so Kanja is not difficult for me. Thus, this would be a very idiosyncratic note that is specifically tailored to my learning journey.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;sounds&#34;&gt;Sounds&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&#34;50-sounds&#34;&gt;50 Sounds&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There are 50 sounds and two intonations (Low and High). 五十音 is the basic Japanese sound chart. Each cell gives ひらがな, カタカナ, its ローマ字 reading, and (in parentheses) the 万葉仮名 source of the ひらがな. Voiced variants appear below their base kana: &lt;span class=&#34;kana-dakuten-label&#34;&gt;゛ 濁点&lt;/span&gt; and &lt;span class=&#34;kana-handakuten-label&#34;&gt;゜ 半濁点&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
